WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:07.360
with Trump losing what is next for the Republican&nbsp;
Party? This is a question I've received dozens of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:14.480
times over the last week. The tougher question&nbsp;
for me about the GOP is what happens in the next&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:21.120
two weeks and two months? Do they back Trump? Do&nbsp;
they all start saying it's time to resign? Does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:26.080
Trump try to do something crazy during the lame&nbsp;
duck? And if so, do Republicans get in the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:33.120
or do they just look the other way or do they help&nbsp;
him do it? The longer question about Republicans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:38.480
seems pretty unambiguous to me. You've already&nbsp;
seen clips. I've played them for you this week&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:44.000
from Willard Mitt Romney and John Kasich. They're&nbsp;
immediately going back to standard Republican&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:49.920
talking points. We have to block Medicare&nbsp;
for all. We have to deal with the deficit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:55.360
which they didn't talk about at all under Trump.&nbsp;
Republicans are going right back to just being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:01.840
Republicans. Once Donald Trump is gone. It also&nbsp;
has to be considered and I dealt with this earlier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:08.160
in the week, I spent 10 minutes, 15 minutes&nbsp;
talking about it. Democrats failed to get a large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:15.680
repudiation of Donald Trump. Donald Trump lost&nbsp;
by thin margins in three to four states. Trump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:22.000
almost got reelected at the electoral level. And&nbsp;
although Joe Biden's popular vote margin is great,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:28.800
Donald Trump got millions more votes than he got&nbsp;
in. Twenty, sixteen, seventy one million votes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:33.360
for Donald Trump. When you get seventy&nbsp;
one million votes for your candidate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:37.920
even if you lose, especially if you think it&nbsp;
was stolen, you're not necessarily going to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:41.600
we need to change everything we're doing.&nbsp;
There may be changes at the state level.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:49.360
Georgia, looking at Georgia, looking at how close&nbsp;
Texas was, you may see Republican strategy change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:54.480
within some states in really particular ways, but&nbsp;
Republicans are going to continue to do well. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:59.760
anyone who says otherwise maybe is not aware&nbsp;
of the history of how quickly the two political&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:06.720
parties bounced back from losses and even from&nbsp;
big losses. This is not a big loss in the sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:12.320
that Republicans are gaining in the House.
They may even remain retain control of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:17.840
Senate. And and Trump got seventy one million&nbsp;
votes. So with Republicans being better at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:22.320
local level, with Republicans having better&nbsp;
ground game generally, but not always,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:26.560
I am not expecting major changes in the Republican&nbsp;
Party. I'm expecting the Republican Party to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:32.080
revert essentially back to what it was up until&nbsp;
Donald Trump became the nominee. I would expect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:38.160
the Republican Party to continue using their wedge&nbsp;
issues, including those religiously tied issues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:43.440
like abortion, less so gay marriage. But&nbsp;
now it's religious freedom to raise money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:48.400
although it may become a little bit more difficult&nbsp;
over time. But I'm not expecting major changes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:53.200
other than a lot of Republicans who were with&nbsp;
Trump for the last four years who will pretend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:58.720
they never were and even start to pretend Trump.&nbsp;
We don't we don't even remember Trump, really.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:03.840
That is what I am expecting from them. So I don't&nbsp;
expect major changes to the Republican Party.&nbsp;

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:09.040
I think the next two weeks and two months will be&nbsp;
key in terms of who's with Trump and who is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:13.520
with Trump And the question of the Senate gets&nbsp;
us directly into question number three today.

